Revision history for Tira

0.65  2026-08-08
    - New `tira.stale` (EPIC-457, the first piece): how long every card
      has sat in the column it is in now, measured from its most recent
      recorded column move, across all three boards in one call.
      `--older-than MINUTES` filters to the ones past a limit. A card
      whose column entry was never recorded is reported with no
      duration rather than a guessed one and never counts as old -
      history only began in 0.53, and inventing an entry time would put
      most of an existing board into the first report. Unreadable
      stamps degrade to unknown instead of failing the board, and a
      column renamed underneath a card leaves its measurement intact.

0.64  2026-08-08
    - Columns now show ten cards with a button to reveal ten more at a
      time, saying how many remain (owner request). The count beside the
      column name stays the column's real total rather than the visible
      slice.
    - The board control gained a keyword filter. It asks the server, so
      a word that appears only in a card's description still finds it;
      the box is debounced, an empty box clears the filter, a failed
      request narrows to nothing rather than quietly showing
      everything, and filtering restarts paging at the first ten
      matches.

0.63  2026-08-08
    - Bulk card moves (owner request): shift-click selects and
      deselects, dragging any selected card carries the whole selection
      to the target column with the ghost showing the batch size, and a
      plain click clears the selection and opens that card. The
      selection is kept by reference so it survives the live refresh
      that rebuilds every card. Each card posts the same validated move
      as a single drag - no bulk endpoint and no new server semantics.
    - Fit mode now runs the board edge to edge, recovering the side
      gutters: on a thirteen-column board the columns go from 81px to
      86px.

0.62  2026-08-08
    - The browser tab now reads `<project> :: <board> :: <cards>`, for
      example `MT5 :: Tickets :: 57`, instead of a generic product name
      on every board (owner request). All three boards on one page read
      `Kanban`, and the count is what the page actually renders, so it
      never disagrees with the board beneath it.

0.61  2026-08-08
    - Fixed cards still drawing over the next column on boards with many
      columns (owner photo, message 3007) — a different defect from
      0.60's, in a different mode. The card list is a CSS grid, and a
      grid item refuses to shrink below its own content by default, so
      once a column is narrower than a card's longest word the card
      overflowed. 0.60 fixed the columns; this fixes the cards. The
      wide-board guard now also runs a thirteen-column board.

0.60  2026-08-08
    - Fixed board columns collapsing and cards spilling into the next
      column on boards with many columns (owner photo, message 3005).
      The table uses a fixed layout, which takes every column's width
      from the header row alone: the headers carried only a minimum
      width, so nine columns were divided into 134px each and the cards
      overflowed. The headers now carry the width itself, so Standard
      keeps its 17rem columns and scrolls as it always claimed to. Fit
      mode is unchanged. New nine-column browser guard, because the
      two-column fixture could not express the defect.

0.59  2026-08-08
    - A leading ~ now means the home directory wherever a path is
      accepted (owner report: answering the directory question with
      ~/foo/bar created a directory literally named ~). That covers the
      guided prompts and a quoted --dir or --project, which a shell
      never expands.
    - Prompts are editable at a terminal: Ctrl-A and Ctrl-E as asked,
      plus Ctrl-U, Ctrl-K, backspace, arrows and Home/End, written
      against core POSIX termios rather than a Term::ReadLine editing
      module, none of which is installed anywhere this runs. Away from a
      terminal the prompt stays a plain read.
    - project.update gained --listen HOST[:PORT] as the compact form of
      --dashboard-host and --dashboard-port.
    - Fixed: pressing enter past the people question in the guided setup
      reported "Every member needs a name" instead of meaning "none".

0.58  2026-08-07
    - A project now remembers its dashboard address:
      `tira.project.update --dashboard-host localhost --dashboard-port
      8080` stores it, project.show reports it, and `-o browser` uses it
      instead of always listening on 0.0.0.0:7899. `any` is the
      plain-language form of every interface. An address given on the
      command line still wins, and both values are validated where they
      are set rather than where they are used.

0.57  2026-08-07
    - New `tira.onboard`: the guided form of project.new, asking for the
      name, directory, people, each board's reference prefix, whether the
      boards share one column set, and the columns, then confirming
      before it writes anything. Flags pre-fill the answers, unusable
      answers are re-asked with the reason, declining exits 1 and
      reaching the end of input aborts — both leaving nothing behind.
      Only onboard ever prompts; project.new stays argument-driven so
      scripts and agents can never be left waiting on input.
    - project.new gained --sow-columns, --epic-columns, and
      --ticket-columns, so boards can differ instead of always sharing
      one set.

0.56  2026-08-07
    - New `tira.project.new` (owner request): one command creates a
      project, its members, each board's reference prefix, and one
      shared column set across all three boards — replacing a
      thirty-five command manual sequence. Column names are given as
      they read ("Done / Release") and slugified automatically with the
      original text kept as the label; columns that already exist,
      including the protected Backlog and Discard, are skipped, so the
      natural list works verbatim and re-running changes nothing. All
      input is validated before the first write, and prefixes are set
      before any record can exist so the first reference is 001.

0.55  2026-08-07
    - Fixed the card dialog's column dropdown showing the card count
      appended to each column name (owner photo, message 2990) — a
      0.54 regression: the option labels were read from the whole
      header cell, which now contains the count badge. Labels now come
      from the column-name element. Card moves were never affected;
      only the visible text was.

0.54  2026-08-07
    - Column card counts and add-card from the board (owner message
      2978). Each column header shows its count, hidden when the column
      is empty, derived from the board so it stays right after refresh,
      drag, and creation. Every column on a live board gains an add-card
      control that opens the dialog in new-card mode: empty form, no
      reference until it is saved, title the only required field, the
      rest optional and editable on the card afterwards. Creation goes
      through the same validated engine path as the CLI and the dialog
      then switches to the created card.

0.53  2026-08-07
    - Per-field history (DD-443): every record write is journaled field
      by field to .tira/history/<REF>.jsonl, whichever command performed
      it, with before and after values, the change time, the operation,
      and an optional validated author. Creation seeds birth entries,
      moves journal their column change, structural fields record that
      they changed without inlining their value, and rolled-back
      operations record nothing. tira.history.list reads it with the
      established window, since, where, count, and truncation
      semantics. History lives outside the boards, so records, content
      hashes, and board reads are untouched.

0.52  2026-08-07
    - Reads are up to 170x faster on mature boards: Cpanel::JSON::XS is
      used when installed, with core JSON::PP as the fallback. Measured
      on 138 records averaging 32KB — dashboard with titles 2076ms to
      12ms, full export 2070ms to 19ms, field-scoped search 2234ms to
      17ms. Output is byte-identical, so stored records never rewrite
      and content hashes never drift; the non-reference tolerance of
      the previous backend is preserved explicitly on every
      value-bearing path.

0.51  2026-08-07
    - Column-width toggle in every board header (owner photo, message
      2976): Standard keeps today's fixed-width scrollable columns, Fit
      all shrinks every column so a wide board — the reported case had
      twelve — is fully visible without sideways scrolling. The choice
      applies to all boards, persists in browser storage, degrades to
      Standard when storage is blocked, and narrow screens keep
      scrollable columns while the desktop preference is preserved.

0.50  2026-08-07
    - The dashboard's default live-refresh interval is now 60 seconds
      (was 5). `?refresh=N` still selects any positive interval and
      invalid values fall back to the new default. With 0.49's
      repaint-only-on-change cycle the board still updates in place;
      it now costs one comparison per minute instead of one every five
      seconds.

0.49  2026-08-07
    - Dialog live refresh repaints only on real change (owner video,
      message 2969): the cycle fetches the record, compares it against
      the rendered snapshot, and does nothing when identical — no
      rebuild, no linked-row refetches, no flicker. The editing guard
      now re-checks after the fetch returns, so a refresh response
      already in flight when an editor opens can never evict it.

0.48  2026-08-07
    - CA21 (owner messages 2951/2953): the dialog's Linkage section
      renders as a table-style list like Attachments — each linked
      record as a row with its ref chip, live title, and column status
      pill, sorted by priority (highest first, unknown last); typed
      links keep their relationship label in a compact typed row.
      Details load through the existing /record provider with per-render
      caching; all linkage edit controls and payloads are unchanged.

0.47  2026-08-07
    - CA18 (EPIC-424): opt-in read-through cache, disabled by default.
      --cache-ttl N enables per call on read commands; entries key on
      the full argument set under the project workspace and stay valid
      only while the ttl and a hi-res board fingerprint both hold, so
      any write invalidates immediately. Hits report on stderr, corrupt
      entries warn and fall back live, cached conditional reads replay
      their exit status, --no-cache bypasses, misuse exits 2.

0.46  2026-08-07
    - CA14 (EPIC-424): -o json is now canonical compact JSON (stable
      key order, raw UTF-8, one line); -o json-pretty keeps the indented
      shape. Identical information, measurably smaller; the live
      dashboard /data payload shrinks with it. t/00's format contract
      updated deliberately with the owner-approved change.

0.45  2026-08-07
    - CA20 (EPIC-424): indexed reads on the append-only gate and
      evidence logs. --last/--first windows (newest last), --id with
      loud misses, --meta-only keeping results/uris/authors/stamps with
      text lengths and annotation counts, entry-level --where
      (result=fail), zero windows and --count as existence checks.
      Annotations ride with their entries; reads never mutate.

0.44  2026-08-07
    - CA13 (EPIC-424): first-class tira.diff. Since mode: added/changed
      kinds with current column, gate, title, new-comment ids, and now
      for chaining. Snapshot mode (a saved full export): per-field
      before/after for scalars, named new comments, explicit changed
      markers for structural fields, and added/changed/removed
      distinguished. Field scoping and --count compose; exactly one
      baseline; never writes. New cli/diff entrypoint (84 total).

0.43  2026-08-07
    - CA19 (EPIC-424): batch reads on show. Repeat --ref or pass --refs
      A,B,C; the response is {records, order, count} keyed by ref with
      request order kept and duplicates collapsed. Missing refs carry an
      explicit not_found marker without losing the call; validation
      fails whole and loud; 100-ref documented maximum; cross-type
      batches; --if-changed refused in favor of the hash-sweep poll.

0.42  2026-08-07
    - CA16 (EPIC-424): server-side --where filtering on list and export.
      Repeatable ANDed clauses: = equality, = empty meaning
      empty-or-unset, != both ways, ~ case-insensitive array containment
      (scalars never match, never crash). Computed fields filterable.
      Unknown fields and operatorless clauses exit 2. Composes with
      projection, count, and since.

0.41  2026-08-07
    - CA10-CA12 (EPIC-424): comment windows (--last/--first, zero as
      existence check), comment --meta-only (body length + attachment
      count, board-wide via record reads), comment-level --fields with
      id kept, attachment --meta-only (filename, real size, content
      type, added time, sha, total_size envelope, newest first),
      attachment --fields/--since/--count, and the selectable computed
      attachment_count record field. The attachment content-type map
      moved into the engine as the single source of truth.

0.40  2026-08-07
    - CA08+CA09 (EPIC-424): the --brief preset (five documented fields,
      title cut at 72 chars, null assignee visible, contradiction with
      --fields loud) and default long-text truncation at 2000 chars with
      ellipsis plus _truncated/_length markers on fields and gate/
      evidence entries; --truncate N chooses, zero omits-but-marks,
      --full restores. Presentation only — hashes come from the full
      record.

0.39  2026-08-07
    - CA07+CA17 (EPIC-424): count-only and refs-only reads. --count on
      list, export, and search returns {"count": N} alone; --refs-only
      on list and search returns a flat deduplicated ref array in stable
      order. Documented precedence count > refs-only > fields with field
      validation still loud; human output prints a bare number or one
      ref per line.

0.38  2026-08-07
    - CA05+CA06 (EPIC-424): content hashes and conditional reads. The
      computed content_hash field (opaque, stable, excludes only
      last_updated) is available through --fields on show, list, and
      export; export adds a board_hash. --if-changed HASH on show and
      export returns an unchanged marker with exit 1 when nothing
      differs, the payload with exit 0 otherwise, and exit 2 on a
      malformed hash; composes with projection and since (stricter
      wins); never writes.

0.37  2026-08-07
    - CA04 (EPIC-424): changed-since reads. show, list, and export take
      --since (ISO 8601, instant-compared across offsets); export's
      envelope adds now for gap-free poll chaining. Future thresholds
      are empty with exit 0, malformed ones exit 2, unreadable stored
      stamps are never hidden, and show returns {} for an unchanged
      record.

0.36  2026-08-07
    - CA15 (EPIC-424): sparse reads. show, list, and export omit empty
      values by default (null, empty string, empty array, hash of only
      such values); --include-empty restores the fixed-key shape. false
      and 0 are never treated as empty; a field named in --fields stays
      visible even when empty; internal callers and the browser dialog
      are unaffected.

0.35  2026-08-07
    - CA01-CA03 (EPIC-424): field projection on reads. show, list, and
      export accept --fields and --exclude-fields (comma-separated,
      repeatable); selection always keeps ref, exclusion applies after
      selection, and unknown or empty field names exit 2 naming the
      offender. Stored records are never altered; the full payload stays
      the default.

0.34  2026-08-06
    - Optimistic concurrency for dialog field edits: each editor remembers
      the value it opened with and sends it as the save's base; inside the
      project lock the engine rejects the save if that field changed
      underneath, the route flags the failure as a conflict, and the
      dialog shows "changed while you were editing" and reloads the fresh
      card. Different-field edits from separate machines keep merging
      safely as before; same-field edits no longer silently last-write-
      win.

0.33  2026-08-06
    - The card dialog header gained a column dropdown beside the ref, so a
      card can be moved between columns from the open dialog like Jira;
      choosing a column posts the same transactional move as drag-and-drop
      and the dialog and board refresh in place.
    - Fixed a live-refresh regression the dropdown introduced: opening the
      dialog auto-focused the new select, which the editing guard read as
      an active edit and suppressed refresh forever. The close button now
      takes initial focus.

0.32  2026-08-06
    - Media previews: video (mp4/m4v/mov/webm) and audio (mp3/wav/m4a/
      ogg/flac) attachments open in a native player; the attachment route
      gained HTTP byte-range support (206 partial content) so iOS plays
      media and seeking works everywhere.
    - TIFF serves as image/tiff and previews where the browser decodes it
      (Safari); browsers that cannot show a clear not-supported panel
      with the download instead of a broken image. Unknown binaries get
      the same panel; office documents remain downloads.

0.31  2026-08-06
    - Escape with an attachment preview open now closes only the preview;
      a second Escape closes the dialog. Closing the dialog by any route
      fully resets the preview layer, which previously survived an
      Escape-close and painted the old attachment over the next card.
    - The open dialog refreshes on the board's live cycle, so changes
      made from other terminals appear — suppressed whenever a field
      editor, comment editor, or the composer is active so in-progress
      typing is never lost.

0.30  2026-08-06
    - Fixed the card dialog opening off-screen on phones when the page was
      scrolled: a positioning override added for the attachment viewer had
      cancelled the modal's fixed viewport centering, leaving users a
      blank backdrop. The dialog now pins to the viewport center.
    - Fixed sideways panning inside the dialog body: the sections area
      clamps to vertical scrolling and long content wraps.

0.29  2026-08-06
    - The dashboard hero shows the project's configured name as the page
      heading, with the product name moved to the eyebrow line.

0.28  2026-08-06
    - Fixed drag-and-drop on iPhone: iOS browsers (all WebKit) kept
      claiming the moving finger for scrolling, so an armed drag's ghost
      never tracked. Once the hold arms a drag, a non-passive touchmove
      listener blocks native scrolling until release; before the hold,
      scrolling is untouched. Long-press callout suppressed on cards.

0.27  2026-08-06
    - Attachment rows show the complete date-and-time stamp instead of the
      date alone, making the newest-first order verifiable within a day;
      sorting already compared full timestamps and is unchanged.

0.26  2026-08-06
    - Attachments render as a true one-per-row list (filename left, date
      right, delete at the row end) instead of a wrapping chip cloud that
      clipped long names on phones.
    - Legacy attachment references without an added_at stamp recover their
      real timestamp on read from the deduplicated store file's own
      mtime, so sorting is truthful for attachments added before 0.22;
      the repaired reference persists on the record's next mutation.
    - Linkage reference chips keep their compact inline form.

0.25  2026-08-06
    - Fixed last-modified sorting breaking after the first live refresh:
      the lightweight placement payload stripped `_mtime`, so rebuilt
      cards all stamped zero and the sort collapsed to reference order.
      The browser data payload now carries `_mtime` and rebuilt cards use
      it; the permanent browser guard asserts newest-first order with
      nonzero stamps after refresh.

0.24  2026-08-06
    - Fixed drops onto columns that already hold cards bouncing back: the
      card list only spanned its content height, so the natural release
      point below existing cards hit dead space and no move fired. The
      drop target now resolves geometrically to the column stripe within
      the card's own board (direct list hits still win; releases outside
      the board still cancel), and the permanent browser guard drops into
      the exact former dead zone.

0.23  2026-08-06
    - Fixed invisible text-attachment previews in dark mode: the viewer no
      longer lets the browser's default plain-text stylesheet render text
      files inside the iframe (white glyphs in dark mode over the frame's
      light background). Text-like attachments are fetched and rendered in
      the viewer's own themed panel with deterministic contrast in every
      color scheme; content stays pure text. Images and PDFs unchanged.

0.22  2026-08-06
    - Attachment references now record `added_at` at add time (file and
      content paths alike); deduplicated re-adds keep the original stamp.
      Dialog chips display the date and sort newest first; legacy
      references without a stamp render an em-dash and sort last.
    - Comments render newest first beneath a collapsed top composer that
      expands on demand with an author picker and a bold/italic/code/list
      formatting bar; comment text is stored as markdown unchanged.
    - Markdown comment bodies render formatted through a DOM-building
      renderer (bold, italic, inline code, bullet lists, paragraphs) that
      never assigns raw HTML, so nothing can execute; `format: text`
      bodies stay plain.

0.21  2026-08-06
    - Fixed card drag-and-drop on touch devices: the board now uses one
      Pointer Events engine for mouse and touch instead of HTML5 drag
      events, which mobile browsers never fire from touch gestures.
    - Touch drags start after a 250ms hold (so native scrolling survives),
      mouse drags after a small movement threshold; a floating card ghost
      follows the pointer and the destination column highlights.
    - The click generated by releasing a drag is swallowed by a tight
      one-shot window, so dropping a card no longer opens a card dialog.
    - The desktop Playwright drag scenario now drives raw pointer input
      (the dragTo helper is HTML5-DnD-oriented) and additionally asserts
      the ghost and drop-target affordances; the mobile pass gains a real
      CDP touch-drag scenario that reproduced the bug red before the fix.

0.20  2026-08-06
    - Made linkage editable in the browser card dialog through the
      transactional engine commands: hierarchy and sub-item parents link
      and unlink, children rows unlink per entry and link new refs, and
      typed links add and remove with reciprocals, with the project's
      configured link types served by a new `/link-types` route.
    - Added `/hierarchy/link`, `/hierarchy/unlink`, `/subitem/link`,
      `/subitem/unlink`, `/link/add`, and `/link/remove` dialog routes with
      structured 422 failures carrying engine validation messages.
    - Moved the long-text edit pencils (description, problem/feature,
      solution needed) into their section headings.
    - Added a phone-width responsive layout: near-fullscreen dialog,
      stacked details grid, and stacked forms, proven by a dedicated
      mobile-viewport Playwright pass with zero horizontal overflow.

0.19  2026-08-06
    - Made every list field editable per item in the browser card dialog:
      labels, affects versions, key details, deliverables, scope included
      and excluded, acceptance criteria, test steps, BDD, and ATDD, each
      with add, edit, and remove controls that send whole-list replacements
      through the engine's validated replace semantics.
    - Added in-dialog checklist management through new `/checklist/add` and
      `/checklist/update` routes wrapping the existing engine commands:
      entries add and edit in place, ids stay immutable, and the
      retained-not-deleted rule keeps any removal control out of the dialog.
    - Scope sides replace independently while preserving the other side;
      linkage remains read-only in the dialog.
    - Array-valued `/update` payloads are strictly validated: list fields
      refuse plain scalars, single-value fields refuse arrays, and list
      items must be plain text.

0.18  2026-08-06
    - Attachments are now first-class in the browser card dialog: chip strip
      with one-click inline viewing in an overlay (images, PDF, and text-like
      files stream through a typed `/attachment` route; HTML is forced to
      plain text so nothing can execute in the viewer), and a named download
      for every other type.
    - Added dialog uploads through base64 with a 16 MB cap, reusing the
      engine's content-hash dedup via the new `attachment_add_content`
      engine method (attachment_add now delegates to it).
    - Added reference-safe deletion: the new `attachment_detach` engine
      method and `tira.attachment.detach` command remove a record's or
      comment's reference and physically delete the stored file, with
      logging, only when no other record or comment still references it.
    - Every comment now renders and manages its own attachment chips,
      separate from the record strip, including per-comment uploads.

0.17  2026-08-06
    - Replaced the browser card dialog's raw JSON blob with a Jira-style
      sectioned view: a details grid, long-text sections, list sections
      (key details, deliverables, scope, acceptance criteria, test steps,
      BDD, ATDD, checklist, subtasks, linkage, gate log, evidence,
      attachments), and threaded comments, with em-dash placeholders and no
      leaked JSON, null, or undefined markers.
    - Added in-dialog editing for single-value record fields; saves post to
      a new `/update` route that runs the engine's validated `record_update`,
      and validation failures render inside the dialog as structured 422
      responses.
    - Added full in-dialog comment management: an author picker fed by a new
      `/people` route (active people only), comment creation, in-place
      editing, and permanent deletion through `/comment/add`,
      `/comment/update`, and `/comment/remove`.
    - Added the `comment_remove` engine method and `tira.comment.remove`
      CLI command; removal reports the deleted comment, updates the record's
      last-updated timestamp, and never reuses comment ids.
    - Unified the browser mutation providers behind one shared builder used
      by both the CLI-launched server and `dashboard.psgi`.
    - Replaced raw UTF-8 punctuation in the embedded dashboard script with
      ASCII-safe escapes so dialog glyphs survive every encoding path.

0.16  2026-08-06
    - Added visible five-second automatic refresh to HTML dashboards, with a
      safe `refresh=N` URL override and a one-second minimum.
    - Added `-o browser` and validated localhost bind overrides for all four
      dashboard commands.
    - Added a Dancer2 PSGI adapter with a full JSON data route; browser boards
      move cards in place through AJAX without reloading the page.
    - Added a Jira-style full-record card dialog and truthful last-updated
      timestamp, plus direct PSGI and Playwright coverage.
    - Added real drag/drop moves backed by the JSON-file column folders.
      Refresh data stays lightweight; complete record JSON loads only on click.

0.15  2026-08-06
    - Added self-contained `-o table` HTML to combined and type-specific
      dashboard commands.
    - Added polished responsive Kanban styling, offline card selection, and
      interactive last-modified/reference sorting.
    - Preserved ref-only zero-JSON reads unless `--title` is requested.
    - Added three type-specific dashboard entrypoints and Playwright coverage.

0.14  2026-08-06
    - Made default dashboard output ref-only without opening record JSON.
    - Added `--title` for title-enriched summary output with one read per card.
    - Ordered every dashboard mode newest-first by file modification time with
      deterministic reference tie-breaking.
    - Retained complete record payloads for `-o json`.

0.13  2026-08-06
    - Accepted Developer Dashboard path aliases wherever a project directory
      selector is accepted, while retaining direct-path precedence.
    - Reused DD's registry/config model without shell execution and prevented
      alias targets from appearing in command results or selector errors.

0.12  2026-08-06
    - Reduced dashboard reads from one full-board scan per visible column to
      one scan per selected board.
    - Preserved byte-identical JSON structure, record order, column order, and
      Discard filtering while grouping records in memory.

0.11  2026-08-06
    - Made hierarchy nodes retain the complete record returned by direct show.
    - Added an explicit Children section to human hierarchy output.
    - Prevented sparse hierarchy nodes from producing warnings and false empty
      descriptions, assignments, priorities, or timestamps.

0.10  2026-08-06
    - Made search and replace `--field` repeatable and cumulative while
      preserving caller-supplied field order.
    - Standardized all search results as `{hits, count}`.
    - Strengthened checklist permanence documentation and added a concrete
      per-field import dry-run diff example.

0.09  2026-08-06
    - Added one-call full project export and explicit full record listing.
    - Added field-aware search and previewable project-wide text replacement.
    - Added dry-run, transactional ref-keyed JSON bulk import.
    - Added stable gate/evidence entry IDs and append-only correction
      annotations without general log mutation.
    - Added symmetric long-form aliases for acceptance and problem fields.

0.08  2026-08-05
    - Made attachment-add responses return the filename actually retained by
      the target attachment list.
    - Added `supplied_filename` and `deduped` response metadata so callers can
      detect filename collapse without rereading the record.
    - Proved independent record/comment deduplication, cross-record filename
      retention, and truthful remove/re-add behavior.

0.07  2026-08-05
    - Fixed silent data loss when updating key details, deliverables,
      acceptance criteria, test steps, BDD, or ATDD arrays.
    - Made included and excluded scope updates append consistently.
    - Preserved explicit `--set-*` wholesale replacement and repeated-argument
      ordering across SOWs, epics, and tickets.

0.06  2026-08-05
    - Added ordered checklists to SOWs, epics, and tickets.
    - Added checklist list, add, and update commands with immutable entry IDs,
      item/status values, timestamps, validation, and Markdown rendering.
    - Expanded the Docker suite while retaining 100% statement and subroutine
      coverage.

0.05  2026-08-05
    - Documented why Tira mirrors Jira's work model while using direct local
      files to reduce agent payload and request overhead.
    - Made CLI argv, comment files, YAML, JSON, and structured output explicitly
      UTF-8 while preserving raw attachment bytes.
    - Fixed long comments containing `£` and eliminated wide-character output
      warnings.
    - Added lossless isolated-byte recovery for records corrupted by the former
      mixed byte/character writer; the next mutation persists valid UTF-8.
    - Expanded the Docker suite to 404 assertions while retaining 100%
      statement and subroutine coverage.

